From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Really Board Support <build@really.local>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 12:10:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] rockchip: really: enable module rails in U-Boot board_init

Nothing in U-Boot probes a regulator-fixed device: the driver has no
DM_FLAG_PRE_RELOC, dm_autoprobe() only walks DM_FLAG_PROBE_AFTER_BIND
devices, and the Rockchip platform never calls regulator_autoset().  Even
with the nodes present in the device tree the enable GPIOs would therefore
stay low for the whole of U-Boot and only come up when Linux probes the
consumers, which happens at ~1.3 s for the I2C4 devices -- before the rail
has settled.  GT9110 (0x14) and HYM8563 (0x51) then NAK and the kernel
needs a userspace rebind to recover them.


Drive the rails from board_init() so power is applied seconds earlier.

---
 arch/arm/mach-rockchip/board.c | 37 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 35 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm/mach-rockchip/board.c b/arch/arm/mach-rockchip/board.c
--- a/arch/arm/mach-rockchip/board.c
+++ b/arch/arm/mach-rockchip/board.c
@@ -336,6 +336,46 @@
 
 int board_init(void)
 {
+	/*
+	 * Really TVI2340R: raise the switched module rails before the kernel
+	 * starts, so I2C4 devices (GT9110 touch at 0x14, HYM8563 RTC at 0x51)
+	 * see a settled supply when they are first probed at ~1.3 s.
+	 *
+	 * These nodes are regulator-fixed with regulator-boot-on, but the
+	 * Rockchip platform never calls regulator_autoset() on them, so the
+	 * enable GPIOs would otherwise stay low until Linux probes the
+	 * consumers.  regulator_set_enable() drives the GPIO per
+	 * enable-active-high, which is what actually powers the two I2C4
+	 * devices.
+	 */
+	static const char *const really_early_rails[] = {
+		"vcc5v0_sys",
+		"vcc28_dvp",
+		"vcc1v8_sys",
+		"lvds_pwr_en",
+	};
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
+		struct udevice *dev = NULL;
+		int ret;
+
+		/*
+		 * These regulators carry DM_FLAG_PROBE_AFTER_BIND, so
+		 * dm_autoprobe() has normally probed them -- and run their
+		 * post_probe autoset -- before board_init() is reached.  A
+		 * second autoset then only reports -EALREADY, which says
+		 * nothing about the pin state.  Enable explicitly instead:
+		 * it is idempotent, always drives the GPIO, and also covers
+		 * the case where the probe is still pending here.
+		 */
+		ret = regulator_get_by_platname(really_early_rails[i], &dev);
+		if (ret || !dev)
+			continue;
+
+		regulator_set_enable(dev, true);
+	}
+
 	return 0;
 }
 

